WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.600
When it comes to making a movie, cooperation
is key.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:07.920
But anyone who's ever had an annoying coworker
knows that sometimes, you just can't get along

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.260
with that jerk you're stuck with.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.920
Considering how poorly Sex &amp; the City 2 was
received, it's a little surprising there were

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:19.500
any expectations for a third installment,
but star Sarah Jessica Parker said she was

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.539
"disappointed" in September 2017 upon the
announcement that Sex &amp; the City 3 wouldn't

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.360
move forward.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.650
Unnamed sources told People that this was
Kim Cattrall's doing, claiming that the star

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.420
was making "outrageous demands" in order to
agree to appear in the third film.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.460
Cattrall, however, denied the claims, telling
Piers Morgan in 2017:

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.240
"I never asked for any money.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.010
I never asked for any projects.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.420
I'm, ya know, to be thought of as some kind
of diva is absolutely ridiculous."

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.980
The New York Post's Paula Froelich wrote an
article blaming Parker and the other co-stars

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.510
for mistreating Cattrall from the beginning
of the HBO series, isolating her early on

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.020
because they were intimidated and jealous
of arguably the show's only genuine movie

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:01.520
star.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.300
After the death of Cattrall's brother in 2018,
Parker expressed sympathy during a red-carpet

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:06.800
interview.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.140
Cattrall responded on Instagram, writing:

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:13.829
"Your continuous reaching out is a painful
reminder of how cruel you really were then

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:15.760
and now…You are not my friend.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.750
So I'm writing to tell you one last time to
stop exploiting our tragedy in order to restore

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.200
your 'nice girl' persona."

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.780
She ended the post with a link to Froelich's
story.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.620
In 1992, Penny Marshall directed A League
of Their Own, about the short life of the

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.100
first professional all-female baseball league.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.060
Well, wouldn't you know it: As memorable as
Geena Davis was in the film's lead, she wasn't

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.900
the original pick for Dottie Hinson.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.780
That honor went to Debra Winger, who quit
the role two weeks before filming.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.180
"You had a little bit of a reputation of going
head to head with your co-stars and kind of

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:47.740
mixing up with them."

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.680
"Wow, this is just going so bad for me."

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:51.340
"No it's going great!"

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.720
Winger, who'd already had a reputation for
not getting along with other cast members,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.590
apparently had a problem with Madonna, who
both appeared in the film and provided its

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.680
theme song, "This Used to Be My Playground."

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.960
League producer Elliot Abbott told the Baltimore
Sun that Winger worried that her presence

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.880
would "cause a circus atmosphere."

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.840
It would be interesting to see how Winger's
career trajectory might have changed if she'd

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.260
put up with Madonna's presence.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.220
In the years since, she's so thoroughly disappeared
from the limelight that it inspired Rosanna

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.940
Arquette to make the 2002 documentary Searching
for Debra Winger.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.890
We'll probably never know exactly what Bill
Murray said on the set of 2000's Charlie's

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.920
Angels that angered Lucy Liu to the point
that he was replaced by the late Bernie Mac

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.330
in its sequel, Charlie's Angels: Full Throttle.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.690
But the basic gist is that while filming a
scene Murray said something questioning Liu's

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.750
acting talent, and Liu responded by throwing
punches at him until the crew pulled them

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:42.240
apart.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.860
"Well, I'm not surprised because I had a real
long talk with a squirrel one time, longer

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.080
than I can talk to most humans."

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.980
In some versions, Murray simply said, "You
can't act."

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.780
MTV offers an account that has Murray pointing
to Drew Barrymore, Cameron Diaz, and finally

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.740
Lucy Liu in succession and saying:

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.060
"I get why you're here.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.160
And you've got talent.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.220
But what in the hell are you doing here?

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.160
You can't act."

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.200
Whatever was said, Murray and Liu reportedly
didn't get along after the incident, leading

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.480
Murray to turn down an appearance in number
two.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.400
OK, so let's get one thing straight: Christian
Bale didn't force Ewan McGregor to quit 2000's

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.400
American Psycho.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.060
They were never castmates to begin with!

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.220
What they were, however, were competitors.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.500
That's right: Bale pretty much strong-armed
the Star Wars actor until he yanked his hat

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.620
right back out of the ring.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:30.120
While good ol' Leonardo DiCaprio was initially
attached to the project, he eventually bowed

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.440
out, and Bale was determined to land the role.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.250
He even refused other roles for nine months
in order to keep his schedule open, even though

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.900
the studio was still looking for a leading
man elsewhere!

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.120
"One part of me wants to take her out, and
talk to her."

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.460
"And what'd the other part of him think?"

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.600
"What her head would look like on a stick."

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.940
Eventually, execs turned to Ewan McGregor,
but he inexplicably gave it a pass.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.200
When GQ asked Bale if he could confirm rumors
that it was him who convinced McGregor to

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.600
say no, he responded:

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.260
"Absolutely.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.460
I phoned a few people and let them know my
commitment, let me tell you!

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.780
I called them all and told them it was my
role.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:04.280
Don't touch.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:05.120
Step away.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.040
Or if you're not going to step away, understand
what you're up against."

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.420
"I'm a pretty, uhh…

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.600
I mean I guess I'm a pretty sick guy."

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:19.430
Which means, from a certain point of view,
you could say Batman used a Jedi mind trick

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.640
on Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:21.680
And that's pretty cool.

